findings.body:
The diff drops the `apiUrl` argument:

::diff{file="ghost/core/core/boot.js" lines="366-368"}

`post-scheduling/index.js` reads `options.apiUrl` straight off the argument:

::code{file="ghost/core/core/server/adapters/scheduling/post-scheduling/index.js" lines="33-51"}

`options` is now `undefined`, so this throws `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'apiUrl')`. Since this runs inside the `Promise.all`, `initServices` rejects and Ghost fails to boot.

Even if the code read the property safely, `PostScheduler` throws `IncorrectUsageError('post-scheduling: no apiUrl was provided')` when `apiUrl` is missing (see ::file{file="ghost/core/core/server/adapters/scheduling/post-scheduling/PostScheduler.js"}).

This change has nothing to do with removing xmlrpc. Restore the original call:

::suggestion{file="ghost/core/core/boot.js" lines="367-367"}
        scheduling.init({
            apiUrl: urlUtils.urlFor('api', {type: 'admin'}, true)
        }),

Without this, `urlUtils` (line 343) is also an unused variable, which ESLint will flag.

The comment says the newsletter and email services depend on the email address service, but its init is now one item in the same `Promise.all` as `emailService.init()`:

::diff{file="ghost/core/core/boot.js" lines="349-353"}

This only works by accident. `EmailAddressServiceWrapper.init()` happens to be synchronous, and array elements are evaluated left to right, so it finishes before `emailService.init()` is called. If it ever becomes async (or someone reorders the array), `EmailServiceWrapper` will read `emailAddressService.service` as `undefined` and newsletters/email sending will break.

The original code had a stray trailing `,` instead of `;`, but it did run sequentially before the batch. Restore that ordering explicitly:

